{
 "slug": "algebraic-notation-and-substitution",
 "title": "Algebraic Notation and Substitution: Writing Expressions and Using Formulae",
 "seo_title": "Year 7 Algebra Worksheets: Algebraic Notation, Writing Expressions, Substitution and Formulae",
 "short_title": "Algebraic notation and substitution",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS3: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Use and interpret algebraic notation, including: ab in place of a × b, 3y in place of y + y + y and 3 × y, a² in place of a × a, a/b in place of a ÷ b, coefficients written as fractions rather than as decimals, brackets; substitute numerical values into formulae and expressions, including scientific formulae.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 7 algebra worksheets: algebraic notation, writing expressions from words, substituting positive and negative values, using formulae, and terms, expressions, equations and formulae. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "3a means 3 × a, ab means a × b, and a² means a × a.",
 "key_sub": "To substitute, replace each letter with its value and use BIDMAS. Put negative values in brackets.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>In algebra a letter stands for a number. We leave out the × sign: 3 × <i>a</i> = 3<i>a</i> and <i>a</i> × <i>b</i> = <i>ab</i>. We write <i>a</i> × <i>a</i> = <i>a</i>² and <i>a</i> ÷ 4 = <i>a</i>/4.</p><p>To <b>substitute</b>, replace each letter with its value, then use BIDMAS. Put negative numbers in brackets.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find 3<i>a</i> + 2 when <i>a</i> = 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>a</i> means 3 × 5 = 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>15 + 2 = 17.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 17</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find <i>x</i>² + 2<i>x</i> when <i>x</i> = −3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(−3)² = 9 and 2 × (−3) = −6.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>9 + (−6) = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Write “5 less than twice <i>n</i>”.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Twice <i>n</i> is 2<i>n</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Then take away 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2<i>n</i> − 5</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Reading 3<i>a</i> as 3 + <i>a</i>, or as a two-digit number: if <i>a</i> = 5, 3<i>a</i> is 15, not 8 or 35.</li><li>Reading <i>a</i>² as 2<i>a</i>: if <i>a</i> = 6, <i>a</i>² is 36, not 12.</li><li>Squaring a negative to get a negative: (−3)² = 9.</li><li>Writing subtraction the wrong way round: “5 less than <i>n</i>” is <i>n</i> − 5.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does 3a mean?",
   "a": "3 × a. If a = 5, then 3a = 15."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is a² the same as 2a?",
   "a": "No. a² = a × a and 2a = a + a. If a = 6, a² = 36 but 2a = 12."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between an expression, an equation and a formula?",
   "a": "An expression has no equals sign (3x + 1). An equation is true for particular values (3x + 1 = 10). A formula connects quantities (P = 2l + 2w)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I substitute a negative number?",
   "a": "Put it in brackets and use the sign rules. If x = −3, x² = (−3)² = 9 and 2x = 2 × (−3) = −6."
  }
 ],
